WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:23.990
<v Ira Glass>OK. Here was the idea. They were private investigators, detectives for hire. But they were not grizzled ex-cops in trench coats or old Humphrey Bogart characters in fedoras dragging on their unfiltered cigarettes. They were moms. Soccer moms. Telegenic, sexy soccer moms. Like some kind of MILF Charlie's Angels.
<v Ira Glass>好的，想法是这样的。她们是私家侦探，受雇的侦探。但她们不是那种穿着风衣、满脸风霜的退休警察，也不是汉弗莱·鲍嘉电影里那种叼着无过滤香烟的硬汉。她们是妈妈。足球妈妈。上镜、性感的足球妈妈。就像某种熟女版的《查理的天使》。

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:33.460
<v Ira Glass>And you could see why that would be a hit. Why people loved that. And why it was really only a matter of time before they would show up as guests on some daytime TV talk show.
<v Ira Glass>你可以看出为什么这会火。为什么人们喜欢这个。为什么这只是时间问题，迟早她们会出现在某个日间电视脱口秀节目里。

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:39.320
<v Announcer>On the next Dr. Phil. They look like your average moms, but they're also--
<v Announcer>在下一期《菲尔博士》节目中。她们看起来像普通的妈妈，但她们也是——

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:48.840
<v Dr. Phil>Gun-packing, hard-hitting private investigators. You would never expect a mom in a minivan to be somebody that's on your tail.
<v Dr. Phil>携带枪支、雷厉风行的私家侦探。你永远不会想到开着小型货车的妈妈会是跟踪你的人。

00:00:48.840 --> 00:01:10.720
<v Ira Glass>The brains behind this whole PI Mom thing was a guy named Chris Butler. The way Chris Butler tells it, he'd been running a regular PI firm, when at some point, he hired a mom. According to Chris, this was the Eureka moment. He realized that moms have what it takes to crack cases. Patience, organization, hunches. Plus, they blend in.
<v Ira Glass>这一整个“妈妈侦探”概念的幕后主脑是一个叫克里斯·巴特勒的人。据克里斯·巴特勒所说，他经营着一家普通的侦探事务所，直到有一次，他雇佣了一位妈妈。克里斯说，这就是灵感迸发的时刻。他意识到妈妈们具备破案所需的素质。耐心、组织能力、直觉。此外，她们还能融入人群。

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.970
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, that was the shtick. Chris called it his secret formula, and he built a business around it.
<v Ira Glass>总之，这就是他们的噱头。克里斯称之为他的秘密配方，并围绕它建立了一项业务。

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:24.160
<v Chris Butler>--5 PM. Charmagne to run on point. That photograph in front of you is when we did recon. That's what the layout of the rooms look like.
<v Chris Butler>——下午5点。让夏曼担任突击手。你们面前的那张照片是我们进行侦察时拍的。这就是房间的布局看起来的样子。

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.190
<v Chris Butler>Inside this bag, we have the recording units.
<v Chris Butler>在这个包里，我们有录音设备。

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:48.830
<v Ira Glass>Chris Butler didn't want to talk to us. This is a tape of him running a sting operation on the hour-long episode of Dr. Phil that featured the PI Moms. And that was just one of several big media appearances for Chris and the Moms last year. There was also the Today show, and Fox and a People magazine article, complete with a two page photo spread of three PI Moms in dresses and heels, posed like Charlie's Angels .
<v Ira Glass>克里斯·巴特勒不想跟我们说话。这是一段他在《菲尔博士》的一小时专题节目中执行诱捕行动的录音，该节目专门介绍了“妈妈侦探”。这只是克里斯和妈妈们去年几次大型媒体亮相中的一次。此外还有《今日秀》、福克斯电视台以及《人物》杂志的一篇文章，文章配有两页的照片，照片中三位妈妈侦探穿着裙子和高跟鞋，摆出《查理的天使》那样的姿势。

00:01:48.830 --> 00:02:01.260
<v Pete Crooks>I was first approached by his PR agent in Beverly Hills. And his PR agent told me that they had just been on the Today show, and in People , and they were just about to be on Dr. Phil , so I should write about them.
<v Pete Crooks>我最初是被他位于比佛利山的公关代理人联系上的。他的公关告诉我，他们刚上了《今日秀》和《人物》杂志，马上还要上《菲尔博士》，所以我应该写写他们。

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:36.900
<v Ira Glass>This is Pete Crooks, a writer and an editor for Diablo magazine. Diablo is one of those glossy monthly magazines that lots of places have, like Chicago magazine, or Columbus Monthly, or Memphis magazine. This particular one serves the East Bay area outside of San Francisco, including the Diablo Valley. The magazine has restaurant reviews, and local events listings, and enthusiastic profiles of celebrities from the area. Pete covers entertainment, so he is the one who got the pitch from the PI Moms public relations person, who told him that Chris Butler and the Moms had just landed a deal for a reality TV show on Lifetime.
<v Ira Glass>这是皮特·克鲁克斯，《迪亚布罗》杂志的一位作家和编辑。《迪亚布罗》是那种很多地方都有的亮面月刊杂志，比如《芝加哥》杂志、《哥伦布月刊》或《孟菲斯》杂志。这份特定的杂志服务于旧金山郊外的东湾地区，包括迪亚布罗谷。杂志上有餐厅评论、本地活动列表，以及对当地名人的热情专访。皮特负责娱乐版块，所以他是从“妈妈侦探”的公关那里收到推介的人，对方告诉他克里斯·巴特勒和妈妈们刚刚签下了一档Lifetime电视台的真人秀。

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:58.730
<v Pete Crooks>I said, you know, we have to do something about this, if this is going to be the Real Housewives meets Magnum PI, and it's all filmed in our backyard with East Bay moms and an East Bay boss. And the PR specialist promised me that I could do more than interview. I could actually ride along on a case and be embedded with the PI Moms.
<v Pete Crooks>我说，你知道，如果这要成为《比弗利娇妻》遇上《神探亨特》，而且全部都在我们的后院拍摄，由东湾妈妈和东湾老板主演，那我们得做点什么。公关专家向我保证，我可以做的不只是采访。我甚至可以参与案件，嵌入到“妈妈侦探”的行动中去。

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:18.220
<v Ira Glass>Which was perfect. He'd be ahead of the curve on this story that was going to be on national TV and had this strong local angle, and it was a fun story. An easy story. One day reporting, where you ride around with the Moms, photos on a stakeout or whatever, which could be great, a quick writeup. And that is the way it started.
<v Ira Glass>这真是太完美了。他能在国家电视台播出这个故事之前领先一步，而且故事有很强的本地视角，又是一个有趣的故事。一个轻松的故事。一天的报道，你跟着妈妈们到处转，在监视点拍照什么的，这会很棒，可以快速写出一篇稿子。事情就是这样开始的。

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:41.150
<v Ira Glass>But Pete ended up spending months on this simple feature story. It unfolded and unfolded, and by the end, he found himself caught up in a very strange world where he was getting mysterious messages from an anonymous informant. For a while, it was unclear who he should believe about anything. And things that were not what they seemed were even less what they seemed than he thought they were when he first discovered they were not what they seemed.
<v Ira Glass>但皮特最后在这个简单的专题故事上花了几个月的时间。事情不断发展，到最后，他发现自己陷入了一个非常奇怪的世界，收到了来自匿名线人的神秘信息。有一段时间，他不知道该相信谁。事情变得越发扑朔迷离，比他最初发现它们不是表面看起来那样时还要复杂得多。

00:03:41.150 --> 00:04:01.040
<v Ira Glass>Today we take you on that same trip that he went on. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Today, The Incredible Case of the PI Moms. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们带你踏上他所经历的旅程。从芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的主题是《妈妈侦探的惊人案件》。请继续收听。

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:07.580
<v Ira Glass>Josh Bearman has been talking to all the available witnesses in this case, and he tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>乔什·贝尔曼一直在与此案所有可用的证人交谈，他将讲述这个故事。

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:20.310
<v Joshuah Bearman>So one Thursday, Pete Crooks goes down to the PI Moms HQ. He introduces himself, gets some background interviews, and meets the Moms and their boss, Chris Butler, the man at the wheel of this minivan full of suburban spy gals.
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以，在一个周四，皮特·克鲁克斯去了“妈妈侦探”的总部。他介绍了自己，做了些背景采访，见到了妈妈们和她们的老板克里斯·巴特勒，这个男人正驾驶着这辆装满了郊区女间谍的小型货车。

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:45.820
<v Pete Crooks>Chris met me at the office. He was Mr. Cool, like right out of a TV show. In fact, I walked in and I saw there were framed 8 by 10 photos of, like, Colombo, and Cagney and Lacey, and Magnum PI, Charlie's Angels, all the '70s and '80s private eyes from television.
<v Pete Crooks>克里斯在办公室见了我是。他很有派头，就像从电视剧里走出来的一样。事实上，我走进去的时候，看到墙上挂着装裱好的8x10照片，有《神探可伦坡》、《卡格尼和蕾西》、《神探亨特》、《查理的天使》，全都是70和80年代的电视私家侦探。

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:58.390
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris actually was an ex-cop, but he parted ways with official law enforcement because, as he described it on his website, his bosses, quote, "had trouble understanding his tactics and drive," which were well above the standards of routine performance.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯实际上曾是一名警察，但他离开了官方执法部门，因为正如他在网站上描述的那样，他的上司“无法理解他的战术和干劲”，这些战术和干劲远超常规表现的标准。

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:38.030
<v Pete Crooks>And then was Chris, who's this kind of puffed-up sort of burly guy with a blond goatee and, like, a very tight black sort of motorcycle T-shirt, tight jeans, and very expensive leather boots. He kind of looked like a biker cowboy cop. His voice just didn't ever change octaves. He definitely had this coolest guy in the room attitude.
<v Pete Crooks>然后是克里斯，他是一个有点浮夸、体格魁梧的家伙，留着金色山羊胡，穿着一件非常紧身的黑色摩托车T恤，紧身牛仔裤，还有非常昂贵的皮靴。他看起来就像一个骑手牛仔警察。他的声音从不变换音调。他绝对有一种“屋子里最酷的人”的态度。

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:51.230
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris sat behind his desk, flanked by his posters and two PI Moms, Charmagne Peters and Denise Antoon. Pete had seen them all on the TV segments. And then, to everyone's surprise, a client showed up.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯坐在办公桌后，旁边是他的海报和两位妈妈侦探，夏曼·彼得斯和丹妮丝·安图恩。皮特在电视节目中见过她们。然后，令所有人惊讶的是，一位客户出现了。

00:05:51.230 --> 00:06:16.370
<v Pete Crooks>Chris sees a a luxury car pull into the parking lot right outside his office. And this woman comes in who has retained Chris to follow her fiance around to see if he's fooling around. She comes in. Attractive woman in her early 50s. And she explains the situation, and tells me how long she's been engaged to this man, and where she met him.
<v Pete Crooks>克里斯看到一辆豪华轿车开进了他办公室外的停车场。这位聘请克里斯跟踪她未婚夫以查看他是否出轨的女士进来了。她进来了。一位50岁出头的迷人女性。她解释了情况，并告诉了我她和这个男人订婚了多久，以及他们在哪里认识的。

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:44.880
<v Pete Crooks>And over the past six to eight months, she's just really noticed that he's becoming more distant, and that he goes to the gym on Saturdays. And he used to go for an hour and a half to work out and come home. Now he's gone for seven, eight hours at a time. And on one of these recent trips to the gym, when he came back, she peeked inside his gym bag, and all his workout clothes were clean and neatly folded.
<v Pete Crooks>在过去的六到八个月里，她注意到他变得更加疏远，而且他周六都会去健身房。他过去工作一个半小时就会回家。现在他每次一去就是七八个小时。最近有一次去健身房时，他回来后，她偷看了他的健身包，发现他所有的健身服都是干净且叠得整整齐齐的。

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:59.900
<v Pete Crooks>She was composed, and then she starts crying and saying that she just needs to know. If there's some funny business going on, she needs to know now. Because this is a big point in their relationship
<v Pete Crooks>她表现得很镇定，然后开始哭泣，说她只是需要知道真相。如果真的有出轨的事发生，她现在必须知道。因为这是他们关系的一个重要关头。

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:09.070
<v Joshuah Bearman>It's like the opening scene of practically every detective film ever made. Nice-looking lady walks into the PI's office with tears in her eyes. Except this one has a PI Moms twist.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这就像几乎每一部侦探电影的开场戏。漂亮的女士带着泪水走进侦探办公室。只是这个案子多了个“妈妈侦探”的转折。

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:22.650
<v Pete Crooks>When the client starts to cry, one of the PI Moms jumped up with a box of tissues, and the other one said, "Do you need a hug?" And they had this sort of bonding moment, the two PI Moms and the crying client.
<v Pete Crooks>当客户开始哭泣时，一位妈妈侦探跳起来拿了一盒纸巾，另一位说：“你需要一个拥抱吗？”她们有了这种联结时刻，两个妈妈侦探和哭泣的客户。

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:34.820
<v Joshuah Bearman>The client suggested the following Saturday for the stakeout. Charmagne and Denise were ready with their surveillance minivan, [UNINTELLIGIBLE] recording devices, and the subject's photo and address. Pete sat in the back, taking notes.
<v Joshuah Bearman>客户提议在接下来的周六进行监视。夏曼和丹妮丝准备好了她们的监视货车、录音设备，以及目标的照片和地址。皮特坐在后面做笔记。

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:42.210
<v Joshuah Bearman>Sure enough, the fiance quickly appeared. They followed him to a 24 Hour Fitness, where Denise and Charmagne videotaped him going in.
<v Joshuah Bearman>果然，未婚夫很快出现了。他们跟着他来到了一家24小时健身房，丹妮丝和夏曼拍下了他进去的过程。

00:07:42.210 --> 00:08:04.820
<v Pete Crooks>Within ten minutes, he came out of that same front door of the gym, dressed up in this sort of Tommy Bahamas leisure wear. Like khaki slacks and dress shoes and a short-sleeved sort of dressy shirt. And Charmagne said, oh, don't we look pretty?
<v Pete Crooks>十分钟内，他从健身房的同一个前门出来了，穿着那种汤米巴哈马休闲装。比如卡其色休闲裤、正装鞋和短袖花衬衫。夏曼说，哦，我们看起来不是很漂亮吗？

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:18.980
<v Joshuah Bearman>And that was just the start of it. They tailed the fiance to a gated community where a young, attractive brunette appeared. She was half the client's age, buffed, glossed, and tattooed up her entire right arm. The couple got in the car and the Moms followed.
<v Joshuah Bearman>而这仅仅是个开始。他们尾随未婚夫到一个门禁社区，那里出现了一位年轻迷人的黑发女郎。她只有客户年龄的一半，身材健美，光彩照人，整条右臂上都有纹身。这对男女上了车，妈妈们紧随其后。

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:25.380
<v Joshuah Bearman>Pete realized they were headed for Napa. The cheaters would spend the day wine-tasting. Classy, Pete thought.
<v Joshuah Bearman>皮特意识到他们正前往纳帕。通奸者要花一天时间去品酒。皮特心想，真有格调。

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:33.160
<v Joshuah Bearman>But Pete started noticing a few things. He was no pro at clandestine reconnaissance, but he did wonder at times about the PI Moms' strange technique.
<v Joshuah Bearman>但皮特开始注意到一些事情。他不是秘密侦察方面的专家，但他有时确实对妈妈侦探奇怪的技术感到疑惑。

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:49.020
<v Pete Crooks>We'd been following him from his house to the gym, from the gym to the gated community, and from the gated community to the freeway, exactly one car length in the same minivan behind this guy.
<v Pete Crooks>我们从他家一直跟着他到健身房，从健身房到门禁社区，再从门禁社区到高速公路，一直保持着刚好一个车距，在同一辆小型货车里跟在这个家伙后面。

00:08:49.020 --> 00:09:09.200
<v Joshuah Bearman>When the fiance turned, they turned. When he made a mistake and had to pull a sudden illegal U-turn, the Moms pulled the exact same maneuver. At one point, the couple stopped at Starbucks. And if you'll just visualize this for a moment, the mommy minivan was right behind them, subject car and tail car, crawling towards iced mochas, alone in a half-empty parking lot.
<v Joshuah Bearman>当未婚夫转弯时，他们也转弯。当他犯错不得不突然非法掉头时，妈妈们做了完全一样的动作。有一次，这对男女在星巴克停了下来。如果你能想象一下：妈妈们的小型货车就在他们后面，目标车在前，跟踪车在后，慢慢地朝冰摩卡开去，在一个半空的停车场里，显得格外显眼。

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:20.910
<v Pete Crooks>And I started to ask, you know, don't you have to hang back a little bit? And Charmagne and Denise said, no, no. You know, he's in his own little world there, on his date. He doesn't even realize we're here.
<v Pete Crooks>我开始问，你知道，你们不需要稍微保持一点距离吗？夏曼和丹妮丝说，不，不。你知道，他在他自己的小世界里约会呢。他甚至没有意识到我们在场。

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:35.700
<v Joshuah Bearman>Did you just think that they were incompetent at doing this kind of thing? Or did you think that they in fact knew exactly what they were doing? When they reassured you, did you say, oh, well, they've done this before?
<v Joshuah Bearman>你当时是觉得她们做这种事很无能吗？还是你觉得她们实际上非常清楚自己在做什么？当她们安抚你时，你说，噢，好吧，她们以前也做过这些？

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:57.930
<v Pete Crooks>I have to admit, I went along with it and thought, they've been doing this five days a week, and I've never gone on a ride along with an investigator before. And I just continued to record notes and go along with it.
<v Pete Crooks>我不得不承认，我顺着她们的意思，心想，她们每周五天都在做这个，而且我以前从未跟着侦探一起行动过。所以我继续记录笔记，跟着她们。

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:06.430
<v Joshuah Bearman>Pete and the Moms followed the fiance and the mistress for hours. They watched them shop for discount jewelry and then move onto a Napa restaurant called the Rutherford Grill.
<v Joshuah Bearman>皮特和妈妈们尾随未婚夫和情妇好几个小时。他们看着他们买折扣珠宝，然后转到一家叫卢瑟福烧烤的纳帕餐厅。

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:25.680
<v Pete Crooks>The subject and the mystery date parked their car. We parked right behind them. They walk right in front of our van. Denise is running a video camera through the front window of the van, and they stop right in front of the van to kiss. I mean, five feet in front of us.
<v Pete Crooks>目标和神秘约会对象停好了车。我们紧跟在他们后面停车。他们就在我们的货车前走过。丹妮丝正透过货车前窗用摄像机拍摄，他们停在货车前亲吻。我的意思是，就在我们面前五英尺的地方。

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.160
<v Joshuah Bearman>So Denise has a camera trained at them.
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以丹妮丝有对着他们的摄像机。

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:39.030
<v Pete Crooks>Point blank, yeah. A big, bulky camera, too. Not some sort of hidden camera.
<v Pete Crooks>近距离拍摄，是的。也是一个又大又笨重的摄像机。不是那种隐藏式摄像机。

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:54.430
<v Joshuah Bearman>It was a lovely day-- the kind of day that brings people out to Napa-- and the restaurant was packed. Turned out the only place for the PI Moms to sit was a communal table, where, as it happened, the fiance was already seated, hoping to feed his mistress French fries.
<v Joshuah Bearman>那天天气很好——那种把人们吸引到纳帕的天气——餐厅里挤满了人。结果“妈妈侦探”唯一能坐的地方是一张共享餐桌，碰巧的是，未婚夫已经坐在那里，希望能喂他的情妇吃炸薯条。

00:10:54.430 --> 00:11:07.700
<v Joshuah Bearman>And so the investigators and Pete wound up essentially having lunch with their targets. Again, Pete wondered if their cover could be blown by being that close. So close that Pete had to say hello to the mistress as they sat down, and then listen to their lunchtime chatter.
<v Joshuah Bearman>于是侦探们和皮特基本上和他们的目标一起吃了午餐。皮特再次怀疑，离得这么近会不会暴露。离得太近了，以至于皮特坐下时不得不跟那个情妇打招呼，然后听他们的午餐闲谈。

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:27.280
<v Pete Crooks>Their conversation was lurid. The fellow kept saying, boy, you look hot today. She says, yeah, I'm hot. I'm sweating. And he says, I'd like to lick that sweat off of you. And she goes, oh, yeah, I'd like that. But like, just without any passion whatsoever.
<v Pete Crooks>他们的对话很低俗。那个男人一直在说，天哪，你今天看起来很辣。她说，是啊，我很热。我在流汗。他说，我想舔掉你身上的汗水。她说，哦，是的，我喜欢那样。但听起来完全没有激情。

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:40.980
<v Pete Crooks>And then she says, I was thinking about going horseback riding tomorrow. And he goes, yeah, where do you go horseback riding? And she says, oh, I've got some friend who has a stables out in Bullinger Canyon. And he says, is that near where we got that hotel room that night? That was a wild night.
<v Pete Crooks>然后她说，我考虑明天去骑马。他说，是啊，你去哪里骑马？她说，哦，我有朋友在布林格峡谷有马厩。他说，那离我们那天开那个旅馆房间的地方近吗？那真是一个狂野的夜晚。

00:11:40.980 --> 00:12:01.940
<v Pete Crooks>So he always kind of turns the conversation back to sex talk, and she kind of goes off talking about horseback riding or whatever. It was very bizarre, and it just sounded like, if this is what infidelity is-- there's just no passion or substance to it whatsoever. And the client back in the East Bay is better off without this guy.
<v Pete Crooks>所以他总是把话题转回性爱话题，她就聊骑马什么的。这非常离奇，听起来就像，如果这就是不忠——它根本没有任何激情或实质内容。东湾的那位客户离开这个家伙会更好。

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:24.800
<v Pete Crooks>Meanwhile, Denise and Charmagne were ordering burgers and ribs, and not really paying attention to the conversation. And we all sort of scarfed down burgers and ribs, and it was just like a very casual lunch. And nobody seemed to be concerned at the awkward situation except myself.
<v Pete Crooks>与此同时，丹妮丝和夏曼正在点汉堡和排骨，并没有太注意谈话。我们大家都狼吞虎咽地吃了汉堡和排骨，这就像一顿非常随意的午餐。除了我之外，似乎没有人对这种尴尬的情况感到担心。

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:44.260
<v Joshuah Bearman>I know. This sounds fake. And that's because it is fake-- fakety fake, totally fake. And the only person who doesn't know it's fake, at this point, is Pete. Everything else-- the mistress, the crying client, the hot, hot horse-ridey sex talk, the affair itself, has been meticulously choreographed by Chris Butler.
<v Joshuah Bearman>我知道。这听起来很假。那是因为它确实是假的——虚假、彻底的虚假。而此时唯一不知道这是假的人就是皮特。其他一切——情妇、哭泣的客户、火辣辣的骑马性爱谈话、婚外情本身，都是由克里斯·巴特勒精心编排的。

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:57.770
<v Sharon>You know, one thing I will say-- Chris was very, very good at reading people. He knew how to read people, and he knew how to push the right buttons. He was very good at it.
<v Sharon>你知道，有一点我必须说——克里斯非常非常擅长解读他人。他懂得如何解读人，也知道如何按对按钮。他非常擅长这一点。

