00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:17.730
<v Joshuah Bearman>Pete's editors assumed the email was phony, written by someone with an axe to grind against Chris Butler. It seemed farfetched that the entire case was an intricate conspiracy and all those people were actors.
<v Joshuah Bearman>皮特的编辑认为这封电子邮件是假的，是一个对克里斯·巴特勒怀恨在心的人写的。整个案件是一场复杂的阴谋，所有那些人都是演员，这听起来太离谱了。

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:29.100
<v Joshuah Bearman>And yet this Rutherford person knew so much detail that Pete had to take it seriously. He called one of the PI Moms, and she swore it was real. Pete was still unsure, so he called Chris and confronted him directly.
<v Joshuah Bearman>然而，这个“卢瑟福”知道的细节太多了，皮特必须认真对待。他给其中一位妈妈侦探打了电话，她发誓那是真的。皮特仍然不确定，于是他给克里斯打了电话，直接质问他。

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:41.760
<v Pete Crooks>And he just-- he screamed, "What! That is BULLCRAP!" And honestly, I could-- I think in text sometimes, and I could see the all caps when he said "bullcrap."
<v Pete Crooks>然后他只是——他尖叫道：“什么！那是胡扯！”老实说，我可以——我有时会用文字思考，当他说“胡扯”时，我能看到全大写字母。

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:50.370
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris promised Pete proof. He could show him it was real. But he never did. Instead, he started dodging Pete's calls.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯答应给皮特证据。他可以向他证明那是真的。但他从未这样做。相反，他开始躲避皮特的电话。

00:21:50.370 --> 00:22:00.300
<v Joshuah Bearman>Meanwhile, Pete and Rutherford kept emailing, with Rutherford feeding Pete information on Chris Butler, and Pete staying up late, trying to track people down on Facebook and piece it all together.
<v Joshuah Bearman>与此同时，皮特和“卢瑟福”保持着邮件往来，“卢瑟福”向皮特提供关于克里斯·巴特勒的信息，皮特熬夜，试图在Facebook上追踪这些人并拼凑出真相。

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:13.520
<v Pete Crooks>About 3 o'clock in the morning, Rutherford sends me one that we've always called in Diablo ever since then the "smoking gun email." And it was the itinerary of our Napa adventure.
<v Pete Crooks>大约凌晨3点，“卢瑟福”发给我一封我们从那时起在《迪亚布罗》杂志就一直称为“冒烟的枪电子邮件”。那是我们纳帕冒险的行程单。

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:20.100
<v Joshuah Bearman>Suddenly Pete was looking at a timeline of his entire day with the PI Moms-- a detailed schedule sent to everyone who was there.
<v Joshuah Bearman>皮特突然看到了他和“妈妈侦探”一整天的时间表——发给所有在场人员的详细日程。

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:49.290
<v Pete Crooks>It had the street addresses of the gym, of the Napa outlet store, the Rutherford Grill, of the winery, and of the Holiday Inn Express. In addition to that, it had a Google Earth photo showing an overhead view of the Holiday Inn Express with notes on it, saying, here's where the luxury car should park. Here's where the mystery date and the fiance will walk into the Holiday Inn Express. They'll walk--
<v Pete Crooks>上面有健身房、纳帕奥特莱斯店、卢瑟福烧烤店、酒厂和假日酒店快捷酒店的街道地址。此外，它还有一张谷歌地球照片，显示了假日酒店快捷酒店的俯视图，上面有注释，写着：豪华轿车应该停在这里。神秘约会对象和未婚夫会走进假日酒店快捷酒店。他们会走——

00:22:49.290 --> 00:23:13.040
<v Joshuah Bearman>Now Pete was certain. He'd been a mark. As it happens, Pete's a big fan of film noir. He knows all the classics backwards and forwards. And he noted that this one didn't just start like all the good noirs, with the dame crying in the PI's office. Pete had arrived at the inevitable turn where the protagonist falls down the rabbit hole of hidden forces and mysterious motivations, where you don't know who's in on the con and who isn't.
<v Joshuah Bearman>现在皮特确定了。他被当成了猎物。碰巧皮特是黑色电影的忠实粉丝。他把所有经典作品都背得滚瓜烂熟。他注意到这不仅仅像所有好的黑色电影一样，从侦探办公室里的女人哭泣开始。皮特已经到达了不可避免的转折点，主角掉进了隐藏力量和神秘动机的兔子洞，你不知道谁是骗局的参与者，谁不是。

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:20.960
<v Joshuah Bearman>Why would anyone go to such lengths to fool Diablo magazine? What was Chris up to? And who was this anonymous source tipping him off?
<v Joshuah Bearman>为什么有人会费尽心思去愚弄《迪亚布罗》杂志？克里斯在打什么主意？这个向他告密的匿名线人又是谁？

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:27.620
<v Carl Marino>I tried to think of everything that could happen, would go wrong or could go wrong. And at one point, I was just like, I have to take a chance.
<v Carl Marino>我试图考虑所有可能发生的事情，会出错或可能出错的事情。有一瞬间，我心想，我必须冒险一试。

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:49.070
<v Joshuah Bearman>This is Rutherford. If his voice is familiar, it's because you've heard him before. The guy sending Pete those emails was Carl Marino, the backup investigator. You know, handsome guy. Black Mustang, sunglasses, dressed to the T, supposedly entertaining a foursome with the cheating couple? That guy.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这就是“卢瑟福”。如果他的声音很熟悉，那是因为你以前听过。给皮特发那些邮件的人就是后备调查员卡尔·马里诺。你知道，帅气的家伙。黑色野马，墨镜，穿着打扮得无可挑剔，假装和出轨的那对夫妇玩四人游戏？就是那个人。

00:23:49.070 --> 00:24:05.240
<v Joshuah Bearman>Carl had met Chris Butler a couple years earlier, not long after he moved to San Francisco. Carl had answered an ad on Craigslist looking for someone with law enforcement and acting experience. Perfect, Carl thought. He was a former sheriff's deputy, and had started doing local acting work.
<v Joshuah Bearman>卡尔在几年前，搬到旧金山不久后遇到了克里斯·巴特勒。卡尔回应了Craiglist上的一则广告，寻找有执法和表演经验的人。卡尔心想，太完美了。他曾是一名副警长，并开始做本地的表演工作。

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:26.700
<v Joshuah Bearman>The two of them, both former cops, hit it off right away. Eventually Carl became a central part of Butler's team. He was given the title "Director of Operations." And he realized that this wasn't the usual PI firm. There were real cases, like cheating spouses. There were performances staged for the media, like the thing with Pete. And then there were all kinds of weird things somewhere in between.
<v Joshuah Bearman>他们两个，都是前警察，一拍即合。最终卡尔成了巴特勒团队的核心成员。他被赋予了“行动总监”的职位。他意识到这并不是普通的侦探事务所。确实有真正的案子，比如出轨配偶。有为媒体精心策划的表演，比如和皮特的那次。还有各种介于两者之间的怪事。

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:41.390
<v Joshuah Bearman>Because that's how Chris operated. Even when people came in with actual problems, he would spin some fabrication around them, layering real over fake over real. He made stuff up when there was no reason to make anything up, And that's how simple situations often escalated.
<v Joshuah Bearman>因为这就是克里斯的运作方式。即使人们带着真实的问题来找他，他也会编造一些谎言围绕着他们，将真实层层叠加在虚假之上。他在没有理由造假的时候也会造假，这就是为什么简单的情况往往会升级。

00:24:41.390 --> 00:24:46.670
<v Joshuah Bearman>One of the best examples of this is the case of the Candyman. Again, here's Carl.
<v Joshuah Bearman>其中最好的例子就是糖果人案。再次介绍一下，这是卡尔。

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:53.620
<v Carl Marino>The mother came in with basically a blue ecstasy pill and said, I think my son's selling drugs. I found this.
<v Carl Marino>母亲带着一颗蓝色摇头丸进来说，我觉得我儿子在卖毒品。我发现了这个。

00:24:53.620 --> 00:25:16.990
<v Joshuah Bearman>This began as a real case when a woman came to Chris Butler with suspicions that her 19 year old son was selling drugs at college. Exhibit A was a pill that his mom found in his room. Exhibit B might have been that his friends called him the Candyman. Anyhow, it could have been a simple job. Follow the kid, videotape him selling X to college students, and the mom will have her proof. But that wasn't enough for Chris.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这始于一个真实的案件，当一名妇女带着怀疑来到克里斯·巴特勒身边，认为她19岁的儿子在大学卖毒品。证据A是她在儿子房间里发现的一颗药丸。证据B可能是他的朋友叫他“糖果人”。无论如何，这本可以是一个简单的工作。跟踪那个孩子，拍下他向大学生卖摇头丸的视频，妈妈就会有证据。但这对于克里斯来说不够。

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:27.110
<v Carl Marino>And Chris basically said, well, we can not only do that, but how about not only do we find out if he's selling drugs, but we set him up in a way where he'll never want to do it again?
<v Carl Marino>克里斯基本上说，嗯，我们不仅可以做到这一点，而且如果我们不仅查出他是否在卖毒品，还设计让他再也不敢做这件事，怎么样？

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:44.550
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris's plan was to have the kid arrested-- or rather, fake arrested-- by Chris and his pals, who would then threaten him with jail time. You know, scare him straight. Incredibly his mom, the client, thought this was a great idea. So Chris planned an elaborate operation over several weeks, starting with phase one-- initial contact.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯的计划是让这个孩子被捕——或者更确切地说，是假装被捕——由克里斯和他的伙伴们执行，然后威胁他会坐牢。你知道，吓唬他走正道。令人难以置信的是，他的母亲，也就是客户，认为这是个好主意。于是克里斯在几周内策划了一个精心设计的行动，从第一阶段开始——初步接触。

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:53.690
<v Carl Marino>He sent these two female decoys to the supermarket. Called the mother. Told the mother, send your son to the supermarket to buy chicken or whatever.
<v Carl Marino>他派了这两个女性诱饵去超市。给母亲打了电话。告诉母亲，让你儿子去超市买鸡肉什么的。

00:25:53.690 --> 00:26:10.630
<v Joshuah Bearman>By decoys, Carl means young, attractive women Chris would hire through Craigslist to play temptresses. The story cooked up by Chris was that these two girls would meet the kid in the grocery store near the chicken, where they would flirt with him by asking for his expertise about the chicken, and then propose a date with both of them.
<v Joshuah Bearman>所谓的诱饵，卡尔是指年轻、迷人的女性，克里斯会通过Craiglist雇佣她们来扮演诱惑者。克里斯编造的故事是，这两个女孩会在离鸡肉柜台很近的杂货店遇见那个孩子，她们会通过询问他对鸡肉的专业知识来挑逗他，然后提议和她们俩约会。

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:12.110
<v Joshuah Bearman>Together.
<v Joshuah Bearman>一起。

00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:36.610
<v Carl Marino>Yeah. Both of them would go out on a date with him together, and I believe they went bowling or something like that. And during the course of this date, they would tell him that there's a party coming up soon that they want him to come to. They would insinuate that they like to hook up together, and how much they like this guy. And they would make out with the guy and stuff, too. These decoys, Chris would find decoys that had no problem doing that.
<v Carl Marino>是的。她们俩会和他一起去约会，我相信他们去保龄球馆什么的了。在约会过程中，她们会告诉他很快会有一个聚会，想让他参加。她们会暗示她们喜欢一起调情，以及她们有多喜欢这个家伙。她们还会和那个家伙亲热什么的。这些诱饵，克里斯会找到毫无顾虑这样做的人。

00:26:36.610 --> 00:26:43.490
<v Joshuah Bearman>Wow. And was it Chris's idea to like, you know, let's make this a threeway, that'll be more exciting?
<v Joshuah Bearman>哇。而且这是克里斯的主意吗，比如，让我们玩个3P，那样会更刺激？

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:55.730
<v Carl Marino>Always. He would throw that in all the time.
<v Carl Marino>总是这样。他总是会随口提出来。

00:26:55.730 --> 00:27:22.450
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris Butler was never the type to worry about gilding the lily. He had his decoys invite this kid to a party hosted by a third girl, a friend of theirs, who, of course, was a lingerie model. They intimated that this party would be so fun, they'd all wind up in bed together. It was a natural progression-- chicken, bowling, group sex at a party with lingerie models. Phase two, a Butler-style twist on the classic honey trap, was nearly complete.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯·巴特勒从来不是那种担心画蛇添足的人。他让他的诱饵邀请这个孩子去参加第三个女孩举办的聚会，那个女孩当然是内衣模特。她们暗示这个聚会会非常有趣，他们最终都会睡在一起。这是一个自然的演变——鸡肉、保龄球、在聚会上和内衣模特群交。第二阶段，对经典的蜂蜜陷阱的巴特勒式改编，已经快完成了。

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:34.330
<v Joshuah Bearman>And with the hook baited, the girls yanked the line. The party is off, they told the kid. It was going to be an ecstasy party, and their dealer flew the coop. Such a bummer, right? No ecstasy, so no party, I guess.
<v Joshuah Bearman>诱饵下好了，女孩们拉紧了鱼线。聚会取消了，她们告诉那个孩子。这将是一个摇头丸派对，他们的毒贩跑路了。太倒霉了，对吧？没有摇头丸，所以我想聚会开不成了。

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:41.180
<v Carl Marino>He's like, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, hang on, hang on. Don't worry about it. I can get this stuff. Really? Can you? Great. Thank you.
<v Carl Marino>他说，等等，等等，等等，等一下。别担心。我可以搞到这些东西。真的？你能吗？太棒了。谢谢。

00:27:41.180 --> 00:27:49.480
<v Joshuah Bearman>I mean, they were talking to the Candyman, after all. And so, just as planned, the kid walked right into Chris's trap, arriving at phase three.
<v Joshuah Bearman>我的意思是，毕竟他们在跟“糖果人”说话。所以，正如计划的那样，那个孩子直接掉进了克里斯的陷阱，进入了第三阶段。

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:54.710
<v Carl Marino>We set it up in the parking lot of the Narcotics Task Force office.
<v Carl Marino>我们把它设置在禁毒特遣部队办公室的停车场里。

00:27:54.710 --> 00:28:07.600
<v Joshuah Bearman>This is a key detail. Carl is referring to the Contra Costa County Narcotics Enforcement Task Force, also called CNET. CNET was the top drug enforcement agency in the county, and the guy who ran it was Norm Wielsch.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这是一个关键细节。卡尔指的是康特拉科斯塔县禁毒特遣部队，也称为CNET。CNET是该县最高的缉毒机构，负责人是诺姆·威尔施。

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:27.530
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris Butler was friends with a lot of cops, but Norm was the biggest. They'd actually been in the same department years earlier. And now Norm and Chris started collaborating on Chris's operations. It was a strange extracurricular activity for Norm, since Chris's operational whimsies were often illegal, like the elaborate fake bust that was about to happen in the parking lot of Norm's police agency.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯·巴特勒和许多警察是朋友，但诺姆是最大的一个。他们几年前实际上在同一个部门工作过。现在诺姆和克里斯开始合作克里斯的行动。这对诺姆来说是一种奇怪的课外活动，因为克里斯的操作奇思妙想往往是非法的，比如即将在警察机构停车场发生的精心策划的假抓捕。

00:28:27.530 --> 00:28:43.470
<v Carl Marino>Chris actually had one of the other investigators on the radio with a script. So when he called dispatch, she was going to be dispatch. Because he was going to be running the license plate numbers, running the kid's name. And she would be calling back like it was a police dispatch over the radio. So it looked very authentic.
<v Carl Marino>克里斯实际上让其中一位调查员戴着无线电拿着脚本。所以当他打电话给调度中心时，她会担任调度员。因为他要查询车牌号，查询孩子的名字。然后她会像通过无线电的警察调度一样回电。所以看起来非常真实。

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:52.050
<v Carl Marino>Chris also had the hummer with the strobe lights on it, the flashing strobe lights. And Norm was going to come driving up in that with the strobe lights going so it looked like a police vehicle pulling up.
<v Carl Marino>克里斯还有那辆带频闪灯的悍马，闪烁的频闪灯。诺姆会开着那辆车过来，闪着频闪灯，所以看起来就像警车在靠边停车。

00:28:52.050 --> 00:29:03.200
<v Joshuah Bearman>So Norm, A real police officer, is going to be driving in a fake police vehicle, but in the real parking lot of the police headquarters.
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以诺姆，一名真正的警察，将驾驶一辆假警车，但在警察总部的真实停车场里。

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:04.150
<v Carl Marino>Yes.
<v Carl Marino>是的。

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:12.060
<v Joshuah Bearman>OK. Just to keep it all straight.
<v Joshuah Bearman>好的。只是为了理清思路。

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:34.790
<v Ira Glass>Remember, this kid was supposed to be a drug dealer. They didn't need to do any of this. They could've just followed him and filmed him. Instead, they now have a real kid selling real drugs to amateur actresses hired off Craigslist for real cash. Then they all get busted by Chris and his buddies pretending to be cops, alongside Norm, a real cop, who's driving Chris's fake police car. Again, here's Carl.
<v Ira Glass>记住，这个孩子本应是一个毒贩。他们根本不需要做这些。他们本可以只是跟着他并拍下他。相反，他们现在让一个真正的孩子向从Craiglist雇来的业余女演员卖毒品。然后他们都被克里斯和他的伙伴们假扮的警察逮捕了，还有诺姆，一个真正的警察，驾驶着克里斯的假警车。再次介绍一下，这是卡尔。

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:45.750
<v Carl Marino>And my job was to run around the front of the car, hold this kid at gunpoint. The other guy was going to go to the other side of the car. Chris was going to come running out of the bushes with the guns drawn.
<v Carl Marino>我的工作是跑过车前，用枪指着这个孩子。另一个人会去车的另一边。克里斯会带着枪从灌木丛中跑出来。

00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:48.610
<v Joshuah Bearman>And now, the guns are not loaded, presumably.
<v Joshuah Bearman>现在，这些枪大概没装子弹吧。

