<v Carl Marino>It's kind of like a mesh pullover thing that you can throw over the side. And we actually made a joke too, about that. That maybe he'd be in his rollerblades on the pier a little bit later.
<v Carl Marino>那是一种你可以穿在身上的网眼套衫之类的东西。我们实际上也开了一个玩笑，关于那个。也许他稍后会在码头滑轮滑。

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:45.650
<v Joshuah Bearman>Yeah. It looks like the kind of thing where he'd come to the door and be like, ma'am, you're under arrest, and then put down the stereo and start stripping.
<v Joshuah Bearman>是的。看起来就像他会来敲门，然后说，夫人，你被捕了，然后放下立体声设备开始脱衣服。

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:52.380
<v Carl Marino>Yes. Exactly.
<v Carl Marino>是的。正是如此。

00:30:52.380 --> 00:31:16.590
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris had two cameras running for better coverage. He wanted to document the day and use the video for his sizzle reel. That's the trailer used to sell shows to TV networks. And it worked. When Chris showed this to Lifetime, part of hooked the network on the PI Moms was the takedown of the Candyman.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯有两台摄像机在运行，以获得更好的报道。他想记录这一天，并将视频用于他的宣传片。那是用来向电视网络推销节目的预告片。而且它奏效了。当克里斯向Lifetime展示这些内容时，吸引该网络关注“妈妈侦探”的一部分就是逮捕“糖果人”。

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:31.870
<v Joshuah Bearman>In truth, Chris was perfect for reality TV. He was a flamboyant character and a master manipulator. Someone who had already been living his life as though he was the star of a show always running in his mind. So it made sense that cable celebrity was the pot of gold at the end of his counterfeit rainbow.
<v Joshuah Bearman>事实上，克里斯非常适合真人秀。他是一个浮夸的角色，是一个操纵大师。一个一直以自己大脑中正在运行的节目明星身份生活的人。所以他在他的假彩虹尽头找到电视名人这个金罐是有道理的。

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:47.190
<v Carl Marino>It was all geared toward getting this TV show and him becoming this Dog the Bounty Hunter-type reality star. I just think he thought somehow that he deserved to be famous.
<v Carl Marino>这一切都是为了得到这个电视节目，让他成为“猎犬多格”那种类型的真人秀明星。我只是觉得他以某种方式认为自己理应出名。

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:54.070
<v Joshuah Bearman>Like any good reality producer, Chris understood the magic of story editing-- how blending some truth with fiction goes a long way.
<v Joshuah Bearman>像任何好的真人秀制作人一样，克里斯理解故事剪辑的魔力——将真实与虚构混合在一起是如何大有帮助的。

00:31:54.070 --> 00:32:26.390
<v Carl Marino>Oh yeah. He was always able to legitimize it somehow. And I fell into that in a few things, too. Because there were a lot of times where things we would do, while not quite illegal, kind of had that dirty, immoral kind of feel to them. And even when I would bring that up to Chris, like, should we really be doing this? He would always find a way. Well, you know, because the commander of the Narcotics Task Force is involved, it's kind of like a police operation. So we are allowed to do this, because he's allowing us to do this, and so it's OK.
<v Carl Marino>噢，是的。他总是能以某种方式使其合法化。我也在几件事上陷入了这种情况。因为我们做的很多事情，虽然不完全是非法，但有一种肮脏、不道德的感觉。即使我向克里斯提起这件事，比如，我们真的应该这样做吗？他总能找到办法。好吧，你知道，因为禁毒特遣部队的指挥官参与其中，这就像是一次警察行动。所以我们被允许这样做，因为他在允许我们这样做，所以没问题。

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:44.720
<v Carl Marino>And he always tried to interweave the whole business within the police organizations. Several different police officers were his friends in different jurisdictions. I mean, obviously the commander of the Narcotics Task Force being the highest level. It was always one of those things where we can always do this, because we always have Uncle Norm who can cover for us.
<v Carl Marino>他总是试图将整个业务与警察组织交织在一起。几个不同的警官是他不同管辖区的朋友。我的意思是，显然禁毒特遣部队的指挥官是最高级别的。这总是那种我们可以随时这样做的事情，因为我们总有“诺姆叔叔”可以为我们掩护。

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:46.600
<v Joshuah Bearman>Is that what he called him, Uncle Norm?
<v Joshuah Bearman>他就是这样叫他的吗，诺姆叔叔？

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:49.130
<v Carl Marino>He did.
<v Carl Marino>他确实是。

00:32:49.130 --> 00:33:15.760
<v Joshuah Bearman>In addition to Uncle Norm, there were other law enforcement types Chris drew into his schemes. Like the real cops he had stinging unwitting people with DUIs, or the retired FBI guys who gave the PI Moms weapons training at a nearby police range. Chris even convinced Glock Firearms to endorse Butler and Associates, getting a bunch of free pistols and boxes of accessories. Another endorsement was Oakley, which somehow became the firm's official sunglasses, and UFC the official gym.
<v Joshuah Bearman>除了诺姆叔叔，还有其他执法类型的人被克里斯拉进了他的计划中。比如那些真正的警察让他用酒驾刺痛不知情的受害者，或者给妈妈侦探进行武器训练的退休FBI特工。克里斯甚至说服格洛克枪械公司代言巴特勒联合公司，得到了一堆免费手枪和配件箱。另一个代言是奥克利，不知怎么地成为了公司的官方墨镜，而UFC成为了官方健身房。

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:28.120
<v Joshuah Bearman>This was Chris's MO. Creating a seemingly valid world with a veneer of legitimate symbols in a kind of lifestyle forgery. And it was all in the interest of self-promotion.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这就是克里斯的MO（惯用手法）。创造一个表面上有效、具有合法符号外观的世界，一种生活方式造假。而这一切都是为了自我推销。

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:48.940
<v Ira Glass>Coming up. We unpeel one more layer of the onion to reveal underneath all the fakery and pretense and self-promotion, real life crimes. Josh Bearman returns with more in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来。我们要剥开洋葱的又一层，揭示在所有的伪装、假装和自我推销之下，真正的犯罪。乔什·贝尔曼马上回来，从芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台继续我们的节目。

00:33:48.940 --> 00:34:16.170
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today we're devoting our entire program to one story, The Incredible Case of the PI Moms. If you're just tuning in, this is a detective agency made of good-looking soccer moms created by a guy named Chris Butler, who would not confirm or deny any of this story for us. His attorney did not return repeated phone calls. The PI Moms themselves declined to speak. Josh Bearman picks up the story where we left off before the break.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们将整个节目致力于一个故事，《妈妈侦探的惊人案件》。如果你刚加入收听，这是一个由漂亮的足球妈妈组成的侦探事务所，由一个叫克里斯·巴特勒的人创立，他拒绝为我们确认或否认这个故事。他的律师没有回复多次电话。妈妈侦探们自己也拒绝发表评论。乔什·贝尔曼继续我们休息前中断的故事。

00:34:16.170 --> 00:34:34.010
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris was thinking big. He had visions of a nationwide network of PI Moms franchise offices and detective schools, all with Carl at his side. And Carl was good with that, until he learned that Chris was expanding his operations into full-on crime. It was around the time the PI Moms started shooting their reality show with Lifetime.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯考虑得很长远。他设想着一个全国范围的“妈妈侦探”特许经营办公室和侦探学校网络，身边都是卡尔。卡尔对此没问题，直到他得知克里斯正在将他的行动扩展到全面的犯罪。就在“妈妈侦探”开始与Lifetime拍摄真人秀的时候。

00:34:34.010 --> 00:35:13.630
<v Carl Marino>Well, at one point during the early filming of the show, Chris Butler brought me into the warehouse, and brought me over to a case, and basically said to me, you live in San Francisco. You probably know people that smoke marijuana. I'm like, I think everybody I know smokes marijuana in San Francisco. It's kind of-- I started laughing. He goes, well, would you know people who would be interested in buying it? I'm like, well, I know people that do smoke marijuana. I don't know who would buy or who doesn't. So he brings me over to this case, he opens it up, and I think there was nine pounds of marijuana wrapped in these plastic bags.
<v Carl Marino>好吧，在节目拍摄初期的一次，克里斯·巴特勒把我带到仓库，带我到一个箱子旁边，基本上对我说，你住在旧金山。你可能认识抽大麻的人。我当时想，我想我认识的每个人都在旧金山抽大麻。这有点——我开始笑了。他说，好吧，你认识会对买它感兴趣的人吗？我心想，好吧，我知道有人抽大麻。我不知道谁会买，谁不会。所以他把我带到那个箱子旁边，打开它，我想里面有九磅重的大麻，裹在这些塑料袋里。

00:35:13.630 --> 00:35:18.130
<v Joshuah Bearman>Carl was taken aback. But even more shocking was his second thought.
<v Joshuah Bearman>卡尔大吃一惊。但更令他震惊的是他的第二个想法。

00:35:18.130 --> 00:35:45.160
<v Carl Marino>When he opened this case, it was one of those revelations where I immediately knew where they came from. Which he immediately confirmed by saying, well, I'm not going to say his name, but you know where this probably comes from. And-- he wanted to refer to him as uncle. And that's where he started-- not even Uncle Norm. Just Uncle.
<v Carl Marino>当他打开这个箱子时，这是那种让我立刻知道它们从哪来的启示之一。他立即确认说，好吧，我不会说他的名字，但你知道这可能从哪来。而且——他想称他为叔叔。这就是他开始的地方——甚至不是诺姆叔叔。只是叔叔。

00:35:45.160 --> 00:36:08.080
<v Joshuah Bearman>It's worth remembering here that Uncle Norm, now cleverly code named Uncle, was the top drug cop in the county. The guy you'd see on TV taking credit when there was a big drug bust where they'd show a table piled high with contraband. And now, Carl realized, Norm was stealing those same drugs from the evidence locker to recirculate them via Chris, who was extending a hand with a tightly wrapped pound to Carl.
<v Joshuah Bearman>值得记住的是，诺姆叔叔，现在被巧妙地代号为“叔叔”，是该县的最高禁毒警察。你在电视上看到的那个人，当有大案抓捕时，他会炫耀堆满违禁品的桌子。现在，卡尔意识到，诺姆从证据柜里偷走了同样的毒品，通过克里斯重新分发，而克里斯正在把紧紧裹着的一磅大麻递给卡尔。

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:21.440
<v Carl Marino>And it was one of those points in your life where you kind of feel almost trapped. And I remember thinking that I want to say no. I've never sold drugs in my life. I'm not a drug dealer.
<v Carl Marino>这是你生命中那种让你觉得几乎被困住的点之一。我记得我当时在想，我想说不。我这辈子从没卖过毒品。我不是毒贩。

00:36:21.440 --> 00:36:43.150
<v Carl Marino>But at that point, I'm also kind of like, this is a situation where I'm already wrapped up in Chris Butler's business. I know so much stuff about Chris Butler. If I say no and give this back to him, it's going to be a personal insult to Chris Butler, first of all. And it's going to be one of those things where I'm kind of scared if I say no.
<v Carl Marino>但在那个时候，我也心想，这是一个我已经卷入克里斯·巴特勒业务的情况。我知道关于克里斯·巴特勒的太多事情。如果我拒绝并把它还给他，这首先是对克里斯·巴特勒的个人侮辱。而且如果我拒绝，我有点害怕，这会是那种事情。

00:36:43.150 --> 00:36:45.440
<v Joshuah Bearman>And he knows you're not on board?
<v Joshuah Bearman>他知道你没参与？

00:36:45.440 --> 00:37:06.920
<v Carl Marino>I know what he's capable of. I've seen what he's capable of. I mean, to the point where he was going to plant drugs on people in the past for clients. And I've watched him set so many people up. I know Chris Butler will set me up somehow, to have some sort of leverage over me, so I don't give up his dirty secrets.
<v Carl Marino>我知道他能做什么。我见过他能做什么。我的意思是，到他过去为了客户而向人们栽赃毒品的程度。我看过他陷害了那么多人。我知道克里斯·巴特勒会以某种方式陷害我，以掌握关于我的一些筹码，这样我就不会泄露他的肮脏秘密。

00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:17.480
<v Joshuah Bearman>Carl takes a deep breath, says he'll try a cousin in San Jose, and takes the drugs home. There he tells his wife, who says he has to go to the police. But Carl has already thought about that.
<v Joshuah Bearman>卡尔深吸了一口气，说他会去圣何塞找一个表亲，然后把毒品带回家。在那里他告诉了他的妻子，妻子说他必须报警。但卡尔已经考虑过这一点了。

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:25.960
<v Carl Marino>I'm like, who I go to? This came from the Narcotics Task Force commander. She's like, what are you going to do? Are you going to sell it? I'm like, no, I'm not going to sell it!
<v Carl Marino>心想，我找谁？这来自禁毒特遣部队的指挥官。她说，你打算怎么办？你打算卖掉它吗？心想，不，我不打算卖掉它！

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:36.550
<v Joshuah Bearman>Well, you're really stuck here. Because you don't know-- now you realize that there are dirty cops at a very high level. And Butler knows people everywhere.
<v Joshuah Bearman>好吧，你真的被困在这里了。因为你不知道——现在你意识到有高层级的脏警察。巴特勒到处都认识人。

00:37:36.550 --> 00:38:07.670
<v Carl Marino>Yes. So we sit and we discuss it for a while, and she's like, well, who do you go to? Do you just walk into the Concord Police Department and say hey, by the way, the PI down the street is selling drugs? And I'm like, Chris Butler knows half the guys in the Concord Police Department. Who knows if they're involved. I have no idea the level that this goes to. I think about going to the District Attorney's office, but I know that Norm knows all those people. He's testified, I'm sure, numerous times. I have no idea who's tainted by this. Honestly, I'm stuck. I don't know where to go with it.
<v Carl Marino>是的。所以我们坐下来讨论了一会儿，她说，好吧，你找谁？你是直接走进康科德警察局，说嘿，顺便问一下，街上的私家侦探在卖毒品？心想，克里斯·巴特勒认识康科德警察局的一半人。谁知道他们是否参与其中。我根本不知道这涉及的程度。我考虑过找地区检察官办公室，但我知道诺姆认识所有那些人。我相信他已经作证过无数次。我根本不知道谁被这件事玷污了。老实说，我被困住了。我不知道该拿它怎么办。

00:38:07.670 --> 00:38:18.040
<v Joshuah Bearman>Carl stalls for weeks. He's telling Chris that they have to wait for the buyer, making things up. Chris puts on the pressure, saying that Norm is getting nervous and they want the money now.
<v Joshuah Bearman>卡尔拖延了几个星期。他告诉克里斯他们必须等买家，编造事情。克里斯施加压力，说诺姆正变得紧张，他们现在想要钱。

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:22.120
<v Carl Marino>So my wife and I go to the ATM that night and take out our own money.
<v Carl Marino>所以那天晚上我和我妻子去了自动取款机，取出了我们自己的钱。

00:38:22.120 --> 00:38:24.550
<v Joshuah Bearman>Carl and his wife take out $1600.
<v Joshuah Bearman>卡尔和他的妻子取出了1600美元。

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:34.250
<v Carl Marino>And I pay Chris Butler for the pound of weed, and just tell him that this money came from our buyer down in San Jose. Which makes him very happy.
<v Carl Marino>我付钱给克里斯·巴特勒买那一磅大麻，只是告诉他这笔钱来自我们在圣何塞的买家。这让他很高兴。

00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:35.520
<v Joshuah Bearman>So you bought it.
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以你买了它。

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:45.660
<v Carl Marino>Yeah. I ended up being the buyer of the pound of weed. Just to kind of satisfy Chris, and just so I can kind of buy a little more time until we can figure out who to go to.
<v Carl Marino>是的。我最终成了那一磅大麻的买家。只是为了满足克里斯，也为了让我能多争取一点时间，直到我们能弄清楚该找谁。

00:38:45.660 --> 00:39:08.060
<v Carl Marino>At that point, we go back to the office. Since I did such a good job, here's another pound of weed. So I have another pound of weed I take my house, and I put it with the original pound in my closet. And my wife's like, what-- I can't afford to buy another pound of weed!
<v Carl Marino>那时候，我们回到办公室。既然我干得这么好，这是另外一磅大麻。所以我又得到了一磅大麻，我把它带回家，和最初那一磅放在一起在壁橱里。我妻子说，什么——我负担不起买另外一磅大麻！

00:39:08.060 --> 00:39:27.980
<v Joshuah Bearman>Carl was only getting himself in deeper. Since he couldn't go to the cops, Carl thought about going to the local paper, but he worried that a reporter on the police desk might ask the wrong cop the wrong question, and tip off Norm and Chris anyhow. So with limited options, Carl took the best one he saw-- the lifestyle reporter from the local glossy magazine.
<v Joshuah Bearman>卡尔只会让自己陷得更深。因为他不能找警察，卡尔考虑找当地报纸，但他担心警察口的记者可能会问错警察错的问题，总之会提醒诺姆和克里斯。所以，选项有限，卡尔采取了他能看到最好的一个——来自当地亮面杂志的生活方式记者。

00:39:27.980 --> 00:39:49.350
<v Joshuah Bearman>That's how Carl became Rutherford, reaching out to Pete and revealing that the stakeout was fake. It was a way for Carl to test the waters with Pete, to see what he'd believe. And when Pete started snooping around, it actually put Carl in a strange bind, because Chris got spooked and asked Carl, his trusted lieutenant, to do some damage control. Again, here's Carl.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这就是卡尔如何成为“卢瑟福”，联系上皮特并透露监视行动是伪造的。这是卡尔测试皮特信任度的一种方式，看看他会相信什么。当皮特开始到处打听时，这实际上让卡尔陷入了一个奇怪的困境，因为克里斯被吓坏了，要求卡尔，他信任的副手，做一些危机控制。再次介绍一下，这是卡尔。

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:59.050
<v Carl Marino>Chris decides, well, you've got to make sure. Make sure you talk to Pete. Give him a call and convince him that everything was real. Like, being the director of operations and stuff.
<v Carl Marino>克里斯决定，好吧，你必须确保。确保你和皮特谈谈。给他打个电话，让他相信一切都是真的。比如，做行动总监什么的。

00:39:59.050 --> 00:40:29.380
<v Carl Marino>So now I call Pete up on the phone-- as Carl Marino, obviously-- and I have an hour, hour and 20 minute talk with Pete, convincing him how everything was completely true. Going through every step of the way, and how, well, even though this might have seemed kind of funny, no, that was really real. Because it really would've-- and I'm completely honest on that part of it, because those are all real things that could have happened. And at one point, I just want to say, Pete, I'm Ronald Rutherford, all right?
<v Carl Marino>所以现在我给皮特打了个电话——显然是以卡尔·马里诺的身份——我和皮特谈了一个小时，一个小时20分钟，让他相信一切都是完全真实的。一步步过一遍，而且，即使这看起来有点可笑，不，那是真的。因为它确实会——我在那部分完全诚实，因为那些都是可能发生的真实事情。有一次，我只是想说，皮特，我是罗纳德·卢瑟福，好吗？

00:40:29.380 --> 00:40:36.910
<v Joshuah Bearman>So you're talking to him as Carl to convince him that it's true, while Rutherford is simultaneously, via email, trying to convince them that it's fake?
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以你是以卡尔的身份和他交谈，让他相信它是真的，同时“卢瑟福”又通过电子邮件试图让他们相信它是假的？

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:37.760
<v Carl Marino>Yes.
<v Carl Marino>是的。

00:40:37.760 --> 00:40:50.440
<v Joshuah Bearman>Right. But then also, as Carl, you do want him to not think that it's Carl, because you want Rutherford to remain anonymous. So you're actually kind of glad to be on the phone with him as Carl, trying to convince him that it's real.
<v Joshuah Bearman>对。但也作为卡尔，你确实希望他不要认为那是卡尔，因为你希望“卢瑟福”保持匿名。所以你实际上有点高兴以卡尔的身份和他通电话，试图让他相信它是真的。

00:40:50.440 --> 00:41:00.460
<v Carl Marino>Yes.
<v Carl Marino>是的。

00:41:00.460 --> 00:41:06.680
<v Joshuah Bearman>So it took a while, but finally Carl trusted Pete enough to let him in on the much bigger, more serious scandal. Here's Pete.
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以花了一段时间，但最终卡尔足够信任皮特，让他参与到了更大、更严重的丑闻中。这是皮特。

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:29.420
<v Pete Crooks>I get this email from Rutherford that says, "I'm hesitant to tell you this. Mr. Butler is involved in some serious criminal activity right now. Butler is very well-connected in the police community and with the Narcotics Task Force I am not sure who to contact about this, and I assure you that it is serious."
<v Pete Crooks>我收到“卢瑟福”发来的一封电子邮件，上面写着，“我犹豫要不要告诉你这个。巴特勒先生现在涉及一些严重的犯罪活动。巴特勒在警察社区和禁毒特遣部队中关系非常好。我不确定该联系谁，但我向你保证，这很严重。”

00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:57.010
<v Pete Crooks>And this started to get at this nagging question I had, which is why is Rutherford telling me all this information? It seemed like it was excessively helpful, as far as just protecting Diablo magazine and myself from writing a fake ride-along story for Chris Butler. But in my mind, I wondered, is there something that Rutherford needs to tell, and that's what it was?
<v Pete Crooks>这开始触及我心中的一个挥之不去的问题，就是为什么“卢瑟福”要告诉我所有这些信息？似乎这在保护《迪亚布罗》杂志和我不为克里斯·巴特勒写一篇虚假的行动故事方面帮助太大了。但在我心中，我心想，是否有“卢瑟福”需要讲述的事情，这就是它了？

00:41:57.010 --> 00:42:12.410
<v Pete Crooks>So I wrote back and asked, what is this criminal activity? And Rutherford wrote back, "Butler is selling large amounts of marijuana along with other drugs, prescription Xanax and steroids, that have been confiscated by the Contra Costa County Task Force."
<v Pete Crooks>所以我回信问，这是什么犯罪活动？“卢瑟福”回信说，“巴特勒正在销售大量的大麻以及其他毒品，处方药赞安诺和类固醇，这些都是被康特拉科斯塔县特遣部队没收的。”

00:42:12.410 --> 00:42:13.920
<v Joshuah Bearman>Again, here's Carl.
<v Joshuah Bearman>再次介绍一下，这是卡尔。

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:32.810
<v Carl Marino>So I don't hear back for a little bit. Of course, now I'm just frantic. Checking my email every ten seconds. Because it's like, where's this going to go? Does he tell the wrong person? I have no idea what's going on at this point.
<v Carl Marino>所以我等了一会儿没有回音。当然，现在我只是发疯了。每十秒钟检查一次电子邮件。因为就像是，这会走向哪里？他告诉错人了？此时我根本不知道发生了什么。

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:40.590
<v Joshuah Bearman>At Diablo magazine, Pete Crooks covers culture and entertainment. He writes a column called Pete's Popcorn Picks. Crime is not his beat.
<v Joshuah Bearman>在《迪亚布罗》杂志，皮特·克鲁克斯负责文化和娱乐。他写一个叫“皮特爆米花精选”的专栏。犯罪不是他的报道领域。

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:53.800
<v Joshuah Bearman>But it turned out that Carl had chosen the right confidant. Pete had a good police contact, a friend who put him in touch with the right people in law enforcement, and a full-on investigation started up. The case got kicked up the chain of command on account of the dirty cops.
<v Joshuah Bearman>但事实证明，卡尔选择了正确的知己。皮特有一个很好的警察联系人，一个朋友让他联系上了执法部门的正确人选，并开始了一场全面的调查。由于脏警察的缘故，案件被移交到了更高的指挥链。

00:42:53.800 --> 00:43:17.780
<v Joshuah Bearman>And soon enough, Carl became an undercover informant. He wore a wire, sometimes two, recording not just sound but video. For weeks, he led a double life. Now he was staging fake drug deals, handing over cash for Chris's drugs, supposedly to resell in San Jose, while state Department of Justice investigators were stationed in the nearby van, or the bushes, or other tactical locations, watching it all.
<v Joshuah Bearman>很快，卡尔成了卧底线人。他带着窃听器，有时是两个，记录的不仅是声音，还有视频。几周来，他过着双重生活。现在他正在策划虚假的毒品交易，支付现金买克里斯的毒品，推测是在圣何塞转售，而州司法部调查员驻扎在附近的货车里，或灌木丛中，或其他战术位置，看着这一切。

00:43:17.780 --> 00:43:36.340
<v Joshuah Bearman>If that's not complicated enough, in the midst of all this, it's easy to forget that the reality show was now in full production. There were two sets of cameras-- one for the DOJ and one for Lifetime-- and at times, they were filming the same people and places. There were so many overlapping storylines it was hard sometimes for Carl to keep it straight.
<v Joshuah Bearman>如果这还不够复杂，在这期间，很容易忘记真人秀现在处于全面制作中。有两套摄像机——一套给司法部，一套给Lifetime——有时，他们拍摄的是同样的人和地方。情节重叠太多，有时卡尔很难理清思路。

00:43:36.340 --> 00:44:03.830
<v Carl Marino>Yes. It was very surreal. At one point, we would film on the one side, where the conference room was, in my office in the front, and Chris's office in the back. And that's where most of the filming took place. And the Video Village setup was in the storage part, which was the other half, where Chris kept all of his equipment. A few of the vehicles were there. And of course, the locked black case that had-- I think at this point it has 12 pounds of marijuana in it-- that the crew members are using as a chair to sit on.
<v Carl Marino>是的。这非常超现实。有一次，我们在那边拍摄，会议室在那里，前面的办公室在我的办公室，后面的在克里斯的办公室。那是大部分拍摄发生的地方。而“视频村”设置在仓库部分，那是另一半，克里斯保存了他所有的设备。一些车辆在那里。当然，还有锁着的黑色箱子——我想此时里面有12磅重的大麻——机组成员正把它当作椅子坐着。

00:44:03.830 --> 00:44:24.400
<v Carl Marino>And I remember walking in there and it's like, wow. How surreal is this? Here's this production crew, filming this reality show in the other room, sitting on 12 pounds of marijuana they have no idea is locked in that case right now. That I'm doing undercover operations for the Department of Justice to sting Chris Butler. I'll be buying those 12 pounds, eventually.
<v Carl Marino>我记得走进去，心想，哇。这太超现实了。这是一个制作组，在另一个房间拍真人秀，坐在他们不知道锁在那个箱子里的12磅大麻上。而我正在为司法部进行卧底行动，诱捕克里斯·巴特勒。我最终会买下那12磅。

00:44:24.400 --> 00:44:44.610
<v Joshuah Bearman>Meanwhile, Chris's main worry was that he was being edged out of the reality show. It turns out that some dude named Chris was simply not as exciting as sexy moms on the case. There was constant petty jealousy and small-time Machiavellian hijinks as everyone jockeyed for attention from the producers. When Chris realized he was not the star, he panicked.
<v Joshuah Bearman>与此同时，克里斯的主要担心是他正被挤出真人秀。事实证明，某个叫克里斯的人根本没有案例中的性感妈妈那么刺激。由于每个人都在争取制作人的注意力，经常出现琐碎的嫉妒和轻微的马基雅维利式的恶作剧。当克里斯意识到他不是明星时，他惊慌失措。

00:44:44.610 --> 00:45:01.330
<v Joshuah Bearman>But the show itself was a disaster anyhow. Cases were falling apart, and everyone-- the Moms, Chris, Carl, and the producers-- blamed each other. Part of the problem was that Chris had sold the show based on fake cases, and now there weren't enough real clients for the PI Moms to help.
<v Joshuah Bearman>但节目本身无论如何都是一场灾难。案例纷纷分崩离析，每个人——妈妈们、克里斯、卡尔和制作人们——都互相指责。问题的一部分是克里斯是基于虚假案例推销节目的，现在没有足够的真实客户让妈妈侦探去帮助。

00:45:01.330 --> 00:45:31.570
<v Joshuah Bearman>That may be where the drug deals came in. One person close to Chris says that he was desperate for money to stage more fake cases for Lifetime and rescue the show. But it was too late. I've talked to people who worked on the program, and I can't use their names, but they said the whole thing was an insane mess. And in early February, Lifetime pulled the plug. The show was over.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这可能就是毒品交易发挥作用的地方。一个接近克里斯的人说，他渴望钱来为Lifetime策划更多虚假案件并拯救节目。但太晚了。我和那些在节目中工作过的人谈过，我不能用他们的名字，但他们说整个节目是一团乱麻。二月初，Lifetime拔掉了插头。节目结束了。

00:45:31.570 --> 00:45:59.560
<v Joshuah Bearman>All this time, the drug deals started to feel more dangerous for Carl. Chris was about to get his hands on some methamphetamine. Carl was getting increasingly paranoid. At one point, Carl says, Chris even tried to set him up, sending an unmarked police car to sit in the alley behind his house before one of the drug buys. The next day, in an I-know-that-you-know-that-I-know conversation, Carl says that Chris basically admitted he had fired a warning shot. But Carl still didn't know how much Chris knew.
<v Joshuah Bearman>一直以来，毒品交易对卡尔来说开始感觉更危险。克里斯即将得到一些甲基苯丙胺。卡尔变得越来越偏执。卡尔说，有一次，克里斯甚至试图陷害他，在其中一次毒品购买前，派了一辆没标记的警车停在他家后面的小巷里。第二天，在一次“我知道你知道我知道”的对话中，卡尔说克里斯基本上承认他打了一记警示枪。但卡尔仍然不知道克里斯知道多少。

00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:16.200
<v Carl Marino>You know, I'm waiting for the other shoe to drop with Chris. If all of a sudden he finds out, I'm going to find myself going on a long ride into the woods somewhere. I've seen him set so many people up. And you'll never see it coming until it's already there.
<v Carl Marino>你知道，我等着克里斯的另一只靴子掉下来。如果他突然发现，我就会发现自己要被带去树林里进行漫长的兜风。我见过他陷害了那么多人。你永远不会看到它到来，直到它已经在那儿了。

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:24.580
<v Joshuah Bearman>By now, Carl had collected plenty of video of Chris selling drugs. But the DOJ only had Chris on camera, and nothing on Norm.
<v Joshuah Bearman>到现在为止，卡尔收集了大量克里斯卖毒品的视频。但司法部只有克里斯在镜头上，对诺姆一无所获。

00:46:24.580 --> 00:46:42.080
<v Joshuah Bearman>Then one day, Carl gets a sudden message from DOJ investigators. They've been watching Chris, and Chris is on his way over to sell Carl the meth. But he's not alone. Norm is with him. This was a surprise. Norm never showed up on these deals, and the unexpected change was suspicious.
<v Joshuah Bearman>然后有一天，卡尔收到司法部调查员的突然信息。他们一直在监视克里斯，克里斯正在去向卡尔卖冰毒的路上。但他不是一个人。诺姆和他在一起。这是一个惊喜。诺姆从未出现在这些交易中，这种意想不到的改变令人怀疑。

00:46:42.080 --> 00:46:59.220
<v Carl Marino>So now at this point, I'm terrified. Why is Norm coming back to the office? And at this point, I think the Department of Justice is terrified also. Why is Norm coming back to the office? I get a frantic text message. "They're coming back. Hide the devices."
<v Carl Marino>所以现在我吓坏了。诺姆为什么要回办公室？此时，我认为司法部也吓坏了。诺姆为什么要回办公室？我收到一条疯狂的短信。“他们要回来了。藏起设备。”

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:02.040
<v Joshuah Bearman>This is from the DOJ people?
<v Joshuah Bearman>这是司法部的人发来的？

00:47:02.040 --> 00:47:16.600
<v Carl Marino>Yes. So now I'm thinking, these guys are coming back to kill me right now. They have found out somehow. And the first thing Norm would do, I know, when he comes in this room, he's going to pat search me, and he's going to find my devices.
<v Carl Marino>是的。所以现在我想，这些人现在要回来杀我了。他们以某种方式发现了。而我所知道的，当诺姆进来这个房间时，他要做的第一件事就是拍打搜查我，他会发现我的设备。

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:30.090
<v Carl Marino>So I'm frantically hiding them, trying to think of what's going to happen now. And I'm the only one in the office. I don't have a gun. Both of them do have guns, and they wear their guns all the time.
<v Carl Marino>所以我疯狂地藏起它们，试图思考现在会发生什么。我是办公室里唯一的人。我没有枪。他们两个确实有枪，而且他们一直戴着枪。

00:47:30.090 --> 00:47:37.850
<v Joshuah Bearman>And so by hiding the devices, also, does that mean now the investigators can't hear you?  They don't know what's going on in there?
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以隐藏设备，这也意味着现在调查员听不到你的声音了吗？他们不知道那里发生了什么？

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:46.310
<v Carl Marino>No, no. So yeah. At this point, I think there's a chance I might die in this office.
<v Carl Marino>不，不。所以是的。在这一点上，我想我可能会死在这个办公室里。

00:47:46.310 --> 00:48:07.430
<v Joshuah Bearman>Carl later learned that the DOJ had put snipers in position, just in case. But Norm, it turned out, was not there to kill Carl, and neither was Chris. It was all a coincidence. Norm had come with Chris that night so they could install a hidden camera in a coffee maker. Why would they need that? Carl didn't ask. He was just glad they weren't that interested in him.
<v Joshuah Bearman>卡尔后来得知司法部已经部署了狙击手，以防万一。但诺姆，事实证明，并不是去杀卡尔，克里斯也不是。这一切都是巧合。诺姆那天晚上和克里斯一起过来，是为了在咖啡机里安装一个隐藏摄像头。他们为什么需要那个？卡尔没问。他只是庆幸他们对他没那么感兴趣。

00:48:07.430 --> 00:48:16.350
<v Carl Marino>One of the biggest reliefs of my life, that moment when I realized, OK. I'm going to make it out of here today.
<v Carl Marino>我一生中最大的解脱之一，那一刻我意识到，好的。我今天能从这里活着走出去。

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:34.060
<v Carl Marino>So they get done talking about the coffeepot, and they're like, OK, you've got the money, right? Let's do this. So I'm like, OK, sure. And at this point, I'm like, screw it. I'm going to film this, too. Because I've had enough of this. I honestly don't want to do any more drug deals. I'm going to get them whatever they need.
<v Carl Marino>所以他们谈完咖啡壶，就像，好的，你有钱了，对吧？让我们做吧。所以我想，好的，当然。在这一点上，我想，去他的。我也要拍下这个。因为我受够了。老实说，我不想再做任何毒品交易了。我会给他们任何他们需要的。

00:48:34.060 --> 00:48:50.750
<v Joshuah Bearman>So Carl went into the other room, retrieved one of his hidden video cameras, and got into position as Chris and Norm weighed out a pound of meth. Carl taped them as they counted out $5000 in cash. Chris and Norm never suspected a thing. Mostly they joked about how the money wasn't in small bills.
<v Joshuah Bearman>所以卡尔走进另一个房间，取回了他的一台隐藏摄像机，并在克里斯和诺姆称出一磅冰毒时就位。卡尔在他们清点5000美元现金时录下了他们。克里斯和诺姆什么都没怀疑。大多数时候，他们开玩笑说钱不是小面额的。

00:48:50.750 --> 00:49:11.370
<v Joshuah Bearman>It was Carl's last performance as a low-level distributor. That night, after it was all over, one of the DOJ cops even complimented him on his camera work. He told Carl it was the best footage they'd ever seen.
<v Joshuah Bearman>那是卡尔作为低级经销商的最后一场表演。那天晚上，这一切结束后，一名司法部警察甚至称赞了他的摄像技术。他告诉卡尔这是他们见过的最好的镜头。

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:26.540
<v Announcer>Tonight the law enforcement community is stunned. A narcotics cop and a private investigator both accused of selling drugs. Commander Norman Wielsch and his friend Christopher Butler were booked into the Contra Costa County Jail in Martinez this morning.
<v Announcer>今晚执法界感到震惊。一名缉毒警察和一名私家侦探都被指控销售毒品。诺曼·威尔施指挥官和他的朋友克里斯托弗·巴特勒今天早上被关进了马丁内斯的康特拉科斯塔县监狱。

00:49:26.540 --> 00:49:47.540
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris and Norm were both arrested the next day. Between them, they were charged with 17 felony counts. They're both facing life in prison. The indictment describes an assortment of other alleged crimes we haven't even heard about yet. Like the happy ending massage parlor Chris and Norm are accused of running called My Divine Skin; and supposedly shaking down prostitutes they'd find on Craigslist.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯和诺姆第二天都被捕了。他们两人共被控17项重罪。他们两人都面临终身监禁。起诉书描述了我们甚至还未听过的其他各种涉嫌犯罪。比如克里斯和诺姆被指控经营的叫“我神圣的皮肤”的快乐结局按摩院；以及涉嫌敲诈他们在Craiglist上找到的妓女。

00:49:47.540 --> 00:49:56.700
<v Joshuah Bearman>Chris was arrested outside the UFC gym, where he immediately clammed up and asked for a lawyer. Norm, on the other hand-- well, here's Norm.
<v Joshuah Bearman>克里斯在UFC健身房外被捕，他立即闭口不谈并要求找律师。另一方面，诺姆——这是诺姆。

00:49:56.700 --> 00:50:08.190
<v Norman Wielsch>I'm taking full responsibility of what I did. I'm not-- again, I did wrong, and I expect to pay for it. I know I'm going to have to go to jail and I've made peace with that.
<v Norman Wielsch>我对我所做的事承担全部责任。我不是——再说一次，我做错了，我期望为此付出代价。我知道我将不得不进监狱，我已经对此感到平静了。

00:50:08.190 --> 00:50:30.830
<v Joshuah Bearman>When Norm was arrested, he started crying and confessed right away. Since then, he's been cooperating with investigators. And yes, Norm took responsibility for what he did. But during an interview in his lawyer's office with his wife by his side, he also laid out what he calls the mitigating factors that allowed Chris Butler to influence, and according to them, take advantage of Norm.
<v Joshuah Bearman>当诺姆被捕时，他开始哭泣并立即认罪。从那以后，他一直在配合调查员。是的，诺姆对他所做的事承担了责任。但在他律师办公室、妻子陪伴下的采访中，他也列出了他称之为“减轻因素”的内容，这使得克里斯·巴特勒能够影响，并据他们所说，利用了诺姆。

00:50:30.830 --> 00:50:54.150
<v Norman Wielsch>Because of my ailments, let's say, I felt inside-- because I was always a big strong guy, you know, a big strong cop. All of a sudden I get sick, and bam, there goes my health. So I'm no longer that big strong-- even though I'm putting up a good fake for people, I'm feeling like a shell of a man.
<v Norman Wielsch>因为我的疾病，咱们说吧，我心里感觉——因为我曾经是个高大强壮的家伙，你知道，一个高大强壮的警察。突然之间我病倒了，砰，我的健康没了。所以我不再是那个高大强壮的——尽管我给人们假装很好，我觉得自己像个空壳人。

00:50:54.150 --> 00:51:05.480
<v Joshuah Bearman>Also, his mother had died. His daughter was sick. He was depressed and suffering from a disease that slowly deformed his hands and feet. I couldn't really picture why that would matter, but they pressed it.
<v Joshuah Bearman>此外，他的母亲去世了。他的女儿病了。他感到抑郁，患有一种缓慢导致手脚畸形的疾病。我真的想象不出为什么这会有关系，但他们坚持这么说。

00:51:05.480 --> 00:51:11.110
<v Lawyer>Show them your feet. Show them your foot. If you were to look at his foot-- I want you see it--
<v Lawyer>给他们看看你的脚。给他们看看你的脚。如果你要看他的脚——我希望你看看——

00:51:11.110 --> 00:51:18.250
<v Joshuah Bearman>Norm didn't show me, but then the lawyer flashed me a photo of Norm's foot on a cell phone.
<v Joshuah Bearman>诺姆没给我看，但随后律师在手机上向我展示了一张诺姆脚的照片。

00:51:18.250 --> 00:51:21.950
<v Norman Wielsch>Over 18 surgeries in the last ten years.
<v Norman Wielsch>过去十年里做了18次以上的手术。

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:48.500
<v Joshuah Bearman>The feet were pretty gnarly. I should mention, Norm really did sound like a guy who realized he had made a huge mistake and was reconsidering his whole life. Trying to understand how he could be so vulnerable to temptation. After all, the money was trivial. Turns out it was only around $12,000. And Norm had a lot to lose-- more than anyone else, probably. But he says it was Chris. He was just so convincing.
<v Joshuah Bearman>脚相当扭曲。我应该提到，诺姆听起来确实像一个意识到自己犯了大错并正在重新审视自己整个人生的人。试图理解他怎么会如此容易受到诱惑。毕竟，钱是微不足道的。事实证明只有大约12000美元。而诺姆失去的很多——大概比任何人都多。但他说是克里斯。他太有说服力了。

00:51:48.500 --> 00:52:17.450
<v Norman Wielsch>Whoever's talked to Chris knows that he has the gift of gab. He could talk you into buying anything. I mean, I'm not blaming him. But he had his way of coercing a little bit more, you know? Where if I would say, hey, that's a stupid idea, all of a sudden the golden tongue would come out, and then all of a sudden I'd be driving home, thinking, oh, that's not a bad idea.
<v Norman Wielsch>任何和克里斯谈过的人都知道他有“三寸不烂之舌”。他能说服你买任何东西。我的意思是，我不是在责怪他。但他有他那种稍微强迫人的方式，你知道吗？如果我说，嘿，那是个愚蠢的主意，突然间金舌头就出来了，然后突然间我就开车回家，心想，哦，那不是个坏主意。

00:52:17.450 --> 00:52:30.870
<v Joshuah Bearman>While Norm blames Chris, Chris blames Norm. Chris's lawyer has pointed out that Norm was the guy with the badge. Norm was the one who signed the drugs out of the evidence locker. The lawyer says Norm was in charge. It was all his idea.
<v Joshuah Bearman>当诺姆责怪克里斯时，克里斯责怪诺姆。克里斯的律师指出，诺姆是那个戴着警徽的人。诺姆是那个从证据柜里签出毒品的人。律师说诺姆在负责。这一切都是他的主意。

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:35.170
<v Joshuah Bearman>Norm says no. Chris was the Svengali, the showman.
<v Joshuah Bearman>诺姆说不。克里斯是那个邪恶大师，表演者。

00:52:35.170 --> 00:52:47.140
<v Norman Wielsch>He was always buying lunch. He always insisted on buying lunch. And he'd pull out his credit card, and then he would bring some of girls there, and he would make sure that they were dressed all pretty and stuff.
<v Norman Wielsch>他总是请客吃饭。他总是坚持请客吃饭。他会拿出信用卡，然后他会带一些女孩去那里，他会确保她们穿得很漂亮什么的。

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:48.360
<v Joshuah Bearman>There's a little theater to it.
<v Joshuah Bearman>这有点戏剧性。

00:52:48.360 --> 00:53:00.560
<v Norman Wielsch>There's a little theater to it, exactly. And then he would come in his black Mercedes. And you're impressed.  You sit and go wow, you know? But anyway, we--
<v Norman Wielsch>这有点戏剧性，正是如此。然后他会开着他的黑色奔驰进来。你印象深刻。你坐下来心想哇，你知道吗？但是无论如何，我们——

00:53:00.560 --> 00:53:14.840
<v Joshuah Bearman>Even now, in his lawyer's conference room, you could hear in Norm's voice that he still feels it little bit. That Chris had some kind of hold on him, as he did on so many other people. It's obvious when you see Chris in action.
<v Joshuah Bearman>即使现在，在他律师的会议室里，你也能在诺姆的声音中听到他仍然感觉到了一点点。克里斯对他有某种控制力，就像他对他周围那么多人一样。当你看到克里斯行动时，这就很明显了。

00:53:14.840 --> 00:53:42.730
<v Joshuah Bearman>Lifetime won't release the PI Moms footage, so all I've seen is that video of the Candyman bust, the one I watched with Carl Marino. My favorite moment is not the bust itself, but when Chris is back in his office, presiding over his crew-- Norm, Carl, the two pretty girl decoys, and various other investigators. The bust has gone off without a hitch. Someone even thought to bring champagne. And Chris is recounting, in vivid detail, the glorious crescendo of his operation.
<v Joshuah Bearman>Lifetime不会发布“妈妈侦探”的镜头，所以我只看了那个逮捕“糖果人”的视频，就是我和卡尔·马里诺一起看的那一段。我最喜欢的时刻不是逮捕本身，而是当克里斯回到办公室，主持他的船员——诺姆、卡尔、两个漂亮的女孩诱饵，以及其他各种调查员。逮捕非常顺利。甚至有人想到带香槟。克里斯正在生动地叙述他行动的高潮。

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:46.240
<v Chris Butler>But it means you're not going to jail right now, so sit down.
<v Chris Butler>但这意味着你现在不会去坐牢，所以坐下。

00:53:46.240 --> 00:53:55.500
<v Joshuah Bearman>They're are hanging on Chris, reveling in his encore performance of the special scenes his team couldn't see, where he and Norm played a little good cop-bad cop with the Candyman.
<v Joshuah Bearman>他们粘着克里斯，陶醉于他对他团队没看到的特别场景的安可表演，在那里他和诺姆跟“糖果人”玩了一点点好警察-坏警察。

00:53:55.500 --> 00:54:17.960
<v Chris Butler>I said, I worked a month on his case, and you're just going to let him go? He's going, Calm down, just do what you're told. So go outside and chill out. And so  anyway, I come back around the corner and I'm just-- you know what? I turn to him, I said, you know what? I've got seven years of following this. So don't even think you can get away with it.
<v Chris Butler>我说，我在他的案件上工作了一个月，你就打算让他走？他说，冷静点，做你被告知的事。所以去外面放松一下。无论如何，我绕过角落回来，我只是——你知道吗？我转向他说，你知道吗？我已经跟踪这个七年了。所以别以为你能逃脱。

00:54:17.960 --> 00:54:36.030
<v Joshuah Bearman>Everyone in that room broke the law, but they were celebrating it like a civic duty. That's how Chris allowed them to see it. Because if Chris had a genius for fantasy, it was that he understood that everyone had their own particular fantasy, and he could spot it and harness it, and weave it together with the rest of the people in his web.
<v Joshuah Bearman>那个房间里的每个人都触犯了法律，但他们庆祝它就像庆祝一项公民义务。这就是克里斯让他们看的方式。因为如果克里斯有幻想的天赋，那就是他理解每个人都有他们自己特定的幻想，他可以发现它并利用它，并把它与他网中其他人交织在一起。

00:54:36.030 --> 00:54:54.660
<v Joshuah Bearman>The Moms wanted to be on TV. Norm wanted to feel powerful again. The media wanted a good story. The Candyman got a little fantasy date. Even Carl told me that before he first blew the whistle on Chris, he hesitated. Not just because he was scared, but because he, too was taken by Chris's grand vision.
<v Joshuah Bearman>妈妈们想上电视。诺姆想再次感到强大。媒体想要一个好故事。糖果人得到了一个小的幻想约会。甚至卡尔也告诉我，在他第一次揭发克里斯之前，他犹豫了。不仅是因为他害怕，还因为他也被克里斯宏大的愿景所吸引。

00:54:54.660 --> 00:55:17.650
<v Joshuah Bearman>It's like something Chris said the last time Pete saw him before he was arrested. Chris was being filmed for the reality show. He was doing one of those confessional interviews, talking to the producer. "The thing about people," Chris said to the camera, "is that they want to believe you. You don't have to give them much, and they'll come right along."
<v Joshuah Bearman>这就像皮特在被捕前最后一次见他时克里斯说的话。克里斯正在为真人秀拍摄。他正在做那些忏悔式采访之一，与制作人交谈。“关于人的一点是，”克里斯对摄像机说，“他们想相信你。你不需要给他们太多，他们就会跟着你来。”

00:55:17.650 --> 00:55:21.440
<v Ira Glass>Josh Bearman in Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>乔什·贝尔曼，在洛杉矶。

00:55:21.440 --> 00:56:02.690
